Z||000011||Fleabitdog||12-01-2001||09:10 AM||||I am a maintenance mechanic at a food packaging plant. About two months ago( the day after the Reading show) I was asked to go check out a mixer because they had no power. The mixer is about 12' long x 6' wide x 5'. You can walk underneath and not touch the bottom with your hands. I figured before I go grab my Fluke and check the fuses that I would just hit the resets. I hit the one and nothing happened. I hit the second and the mixer started. I turned off the mixer, shut the door to the control box and hit the start button to make sure it would start on its own. It did I shut it off and told the supervisor it was ok to use. Abou an hour or so later it was discovered that a fellow worker had been inside the mixer cleaning it. He was killed. It took the emergecy people over two hours to get him out. They finally started the mixer up in reverse to get him out. My company had no lockout devices and the supervisor told me some time after that it was the workers fault because he was told to pull the fuses before he went in the mixer. For one thing that is not how you are supposed to lock out a piece of machinery. Second, the guy was on a work release program from jail and I feel nobody other an authorized worker such as a mechanic should be putting thier fingers inside a box with 220v or even 110v. And thirdly after talking wit OSHA I went up and for the first time went up the ladder to the top of the mixer. There are two doors on the very top that lift up. Also there are two micro safety switches under the doors. When the door, either one is open the micro safety switches open the circuit so the power is cut off. Both switches only had about 1 1/2" wire sticking out of the sealtite fitting. They were cut off. The way the company works is they buy used machinery and just get it running. Safety does not matter to them. I have been off work on Doctors orders and workmans comp does not want to pay me. I have a lawyer fighting it now. I don't beleive I can work around machines again. My therapist agrees. The worker that was killed was a black man with 8 children and 8 grandchildren. He was the nicest person in the place. I tried working there for about two weeks after the accident. It was preety bad and There were times I had to leave. One Friday they wanted me to get a machine ready for production. It is about ten foot tall and the person that fills the product into the filler has to stand on top. If he woud step 16' either way he would fall to the cement floor. I told my supervisor that I wanted no part of the line as long as someone was on top of the machine. He told the owner. Well the owner came over with a 1/4" nylon rope and tied it over the workers waist, got him on top of the machine and tied the other end to a rafter, looked at me and said safety line. I lost it. I left work that day and have not been back. I felt a lot of guilt for awhile, I should have climbed up and looked inside the mixer. But now I am feeling more anger at the company. It was thier negligence that resulted in his death and then to tie a worker to the roof, that is sick. Plus 90% of the workers are work release or from an agency in Philly that supplies Asians and 98% of them can't even speak English but they work around dangerous machinery and are treated like dogs. Sorry so long I have not told this story a lot and it feels good to get it out. I pray for Smitty and his family every night and even during the day. He comes into my mind quite often. But I wish the owner of the company would burn in Hell!!!! Thanks  Dave  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fierowrecker||12-01-2001||01:29 PM||||Hey Dave...<BR>Sorry to hear of the accident...<BR>If you truly want that place shut down, tell EVERYONE that story...<BR>Send it to the local news stations, shout it from the roof tops, whatever it takes to close down such an operation...<BR>Do you mind if I copy your story?<BR>I need written permission to use it, and I need names, dates, etc...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000013||Raydar||12-01-2001||01:46 PM||||Wow, Dave.<BR> <BR>That's sad. I understand where you are coming from about the safety thing though. I was almost electrocuted on top of a freaking mountain, working on a radio transmitter, because someone left the high voltage cages off.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><BR>I work for the power company, now. <BR><I>Anything</I> life threatening that gets worked on, gets powered down and the breaker gets tagged. <P>You shouldn't feel any guilt for this situation. Your employer set the stage, by his (IMHO) negligent actions. If it hadn't been this, I can assure you it would have been something else, sooner or later. <P>Yeah. I'd tell your story to anybody who will listen.<BR>I'm amazed that OSHA has cleaned their clock, already. <P>Peace. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-01-2001).]||||reg||1
